{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792310570,"nonce":"kzBtAwTYEBXr1N8XHaVZSCelY/A=","title":"FIXED (commit b6f588b) — symptom looked like \"cross-module dispatch silently breaks when imported class's other methods grow\". Actual root ...","message":"Cross-file instance method on a user class silently failed to dispatch (no trace, no error, no call in MIR dump). Bisected to a specific trigger: adding ONE more call site to `readFloatOr(reader, key, 1e-5)` in the imported class's `metadataFromReader` broke calls to `ping()` (a totally unrelated method) from the importing file. Looked exactly like MIR function-ID renumbering corruption.\n\n\nTHREE bugs masquerading as one (chronological order found):\n\n\n`parser/src/lexer.rs::lex_number` only consumed `e[+-]?digits` when the number ALREADY had a fractional part. `1.0e-5` worked; `1e-5` lexed as `IntLit(1)` `Ident(\"e\")` `Minus` `IntLit(5)`. Type checker reported `Cannot find name 'e'`. **Fix**: hoist the exponent scan out of the fractional-part block. Lookahead requires a digit after the optional sign so `var e = ...` still lexes as Ident.\n\n\n`compilation.rs::try_compile_import` returned `false` on TAST/HIR errors and discarded the error list — only `debug!` log saw it. The runner retried once and gave up. Result: a typo in an imported file produced an empty MIR module for that import; call sites in user code landed on forward-ref stubs that ended in `unreachable`, surfacing as SIGILL or clean exit-0 at unrelated call sites with no clue which file actually failed.\n\n**Fix**: convert each `CompilationError` to a `Diagnostic`, push to `collected_diagnostics`. The retry pass uses a snapshot+truncate pattern so transient ordering failures that retry resolves don't surface as noise.\n\n\nHaxe lets a switch arm list multiple values with commas: `case 12, 14:` matches both 12 and 14. The RD parser expected a single pattern followed by `:` — the comma raised \"expected Colon, found Comma\" mid-class-body, which the parser handled by bailing out of the enclosing class. Every declaration after the offending switch silently vanished from the AST. `nue/nue/loader/GGUFLoader.hx::decodeTensor` has `case 12, 14:` for Q4_K/Q4_K_M dtypes → the entire `GGUFLoader` class dropped from the AST → downstream type checker created forward-ref stubs for every cross-file call → stubs lowered to `unreachable` MIR → callers silently SIGILL'd or exit-0'd.\n\n**Fix**: in `parser/src/rd/expr.rs::parse_switch_expression`, after the first pattern loop on `,` collecting alternatives and wrap them in `Pattern::Or`. TAST/MIR already handles `Pattern::Or` correctly (committed earlier in 3227708 + 6a2210b).\n\nAlso commit 211f050 includes `renumber_and_push_import_mir` populating `stdlib_function_name_map` with each import's qualified-name entries — previously only the BLADE-cache hit path did this, so fresh-compiled user packages were unreachable by qualified name from callers in other files.\n\n\nThe fragile reproducer was \"imported class with `1e-5` somewhere in its body\". Removing OR adding methods that contained `1e-5` flipped the symptom because each method that contained scientific notation triggered the parse-error → silent-import-drop → forward-ref-stub chain. It LOOKED like a function-count renumbering issue because that's the most obvious explanation when \"more imported methods → unrelated call breaks\", but the actual mechanism was much simpler.\n\n\nWhen a cross-file method silently disappears:\n1. With this fix, the user now sees the underlying import error directly. Always read the error before bisecting.\n2. If the error mentions an identifier name (`Cannot find name 'e'`), check for scientific notation, regex literals, or other lexer-edge-case constructs in the imported file.\n3. The original \"cross-module dispatch bug\" theory was wrong. MIR function-ID renumbering works correctly. Don't chase that.\n\n\n- `/tmp/bug_repro` minimal reproducer (class L with `1e-5` in a helper) now executes ping correctly.\n- `/tmp/scinotation.hx` covers `1e5`, `1e-5`, `1E5`, `1E+5`, `2.5e3`, `2.5E-3`, `1.0`, `1`, plus an identifier `var ee = 7`. All parse to the correct token / literal type.\n- Haxe suite stable at 128/128 in steady state.\n\n\nllama-chat through `GGUFLoader` now exposes a *separate* error: `Cannot access field 'hex': class not registered or field does not exist` for `nue.loader.GGUFReader.MetaValue`. That's an enum-from-class-namespace import issue, unrelated to the scientific notation fix. Track separately.\n\n---\nArchived from Claude memory `bugs_cross_module_dispatch_complexity` on 2026-10-18. A record of past work; open items in it may have their own issues.","files":null}]}